 "findings": {
  "b3_is_the_fastest_and_it_is_new": "R3 (B3, length-sorted) pooled 492.553s against R4's 501.018s and R2's 505.523s. v1's single ordered pass had put R4 marginally ahead; counterbalancing reorders them. B3 had never been measured before v2 and it wins on BOTH axes -- fastest and lower VRAM than R4 -- so the near-tie rule confirms the choice rather than overriding it.",
  "position_was_not_the_confound": "round-to-round variation was 0.04-0.12% for every protocol, and R4 ran position 4 then position 1 for 250.41s and 250.61s. On this card position is worth ~0.1%, so v1's 0.76% gap was real but small. Counterbalancing did not correct a bias; it produced a clean enough number to reorder two candidates that were genuinely close.",
  "the_screen_predicted_the_full_scorer_to_0_2_percent": "screen 1.1908x, full 1.1884x. A three-layer screen over all items and all heads is an excellent and cheap predictor of the full-scorer ratio on this workload.",
  "the_comparability_rule_changed_the_sign_of_the_answer": "the fresh full B1 measured 2720.66s against the prior session's 2190.17s -- 1.242x, matching the 1.2467x the v1 screen predicted. Using the stale reference would have given 2190.17/2289.37 = 0.9567x, i.e. it would have reported the candidate as SLOWER than B1. The +/-5% rule did not merely tighten a number; it prevented a wrong conclusion in the opposite direction.",
  "adoption": "1.1884x against a 1.25x gate. Length-aware packing is worth about 19% of a full causal-KL scorer on this workload -- real, reproducible, and below the threshold set for adopting it."
 },
